import Mathlib.RingTheory.Algebraic.StronglyTranscendental
import Sheaf.RingTheory.QuasiFiniteAtPrime

/-!
# A transcendental generator prevents quasi-finiteness for domains

Source: Stacks Project, Algebra, Lemma 10.123.9 (Tag 00Q1)
(blueprint: lem-weil-pbc-domains-strongly-transcendental-not-quasi-finite).
-/

universe u v

namespace Algebra

/-- (blueprint: lem-weil-pbc-domains-strongly-transcendental-not-quasi-finite)
Stacks Project, Algebra, Lemma 10.123.9 (Tag 00Q1): for an inclusion of domains
`R ⊆ S`, if `x` is strongly transcendental over `R` and `S` is finite over `R[x]`,
the inclusion is not quasi-finite at any prime. `FaithfulSMul R S` expresses that
the structure map is injective, and `Algebra.adjoin R {x}` is the subalgebra `R[x]`.
The finite-type structure in the conclusion follows from the module-finiteness hypothesis.

Proof, following the ten steps of the node:
1. Module generators over `R[x]`, together with `x`, generate `S` as an `R`-algebra.
Strong transcendence and injectivity identify `R[x]` with the polynomial ring.
2. First suppose `R` is normal and the map is quasi-finite at `q`. The isolated fibre
prime has residue field finite over `κ(p)`, where `p = q ∩ R`, by
`lem-weil-pbc-isolated-prime-finite-type-algebra-residue-finite` and
`lem-weil-pbc-fibre-ring-primes-residue-fields`.
3. For `r = q ∩ R[x]`, the intermediate residue field `κ(r)` is also finite over `κ(p)`.
4. Linear dependence of the powers of the image of `x` in `κ(r)`, followed by clearing
 denominators, gives a polynomial in `r` with a coefficient outside `p`. Thus
`pR[x] < r`; the former is prime by `lem-weil-pbc-polynomial-ring-domain`.
5. The ring `R[x]` is normal by `lem-weil-pbc-polynomial-ring-normal-domain`, and
`S` is integral over it by `lem-weil-pbc-finite-algebra-is-integral`. Apply
`lem-weil-pbc-going-down-normal-integral` to obtain `q₁ < q` over `pR[x]`.
6. Both primes lie over `p`. The inclusion-preserving fibre-prime correspondence
`lem-weil-pbc-fibre-ring-primes-bijection` shows that the fibre point of `q` has a
strict generalization, so its singleton is not open. This proves the normal case.
7. In general, take fraction fields `K ⊆ L`, the normalization `R' ⊆ K` of `R`,
and the subring `S' ⊆ L` generated by `R'` and `S`. The node
`lem-weil-pbc-integral-closure-algebraic-extension-normal` gives normality of `R'`.
The map `S ⊗[R] R' → S'` is surjective by its construction.
8. Clearing denominators proves strong transcendence of `x` over `R'`. The original
module generators still generate `S'` over `R'[x]`, so the normal case applies.
9. The ring `S'` is integral over `S`. By
`lem-weil-pbc-integral-injective-lying-over`, every prime of `S` lifts to `S'`.
10. If the original map were quasi-finite at any prime, apply
`lem-weil-pbc-quasi-finite-four-rings` to a lifted prime. It would make `R' → S'`
quasi-finite there, contradicting step 8. -/
theorem not_isQuasiFiniteAtPrime_of_isStronglyTranscendental
    (R : Type u) (S : Type v) [CommRing R] [CommRing S]
    [IsDomain R] [IsDomain S] [Algebra R S] [FaithfulSMul R S]
    (x : S) (hx : IsStronglyTranscendental R x)
    [Module.Finite (Algebra.adjoin R ({x} : Set S)) S] :
    letI : Algebra.FiniteType R S :=
      Algebra.FiniteType.trans
        (Algebra.FiniteType.adjoin_of_finite (Set.finite_singleton x))
        (inferInstance : Algebra.FiniteType (Algebra.adjoin R ({x} : Set S)) S)
    ∀ q : PrimeSpectrum S, ¬ IsQuasiFiniteAtPrime R S q := by
  sorry

end Algebra
